  "prediction": "The report provides an extensive list of resources and contact information for various government agencies and offices that offer services and information relevant to congressional offices. These resources include libraries, research institutions, budget and accounting offices, and executive branch departments.\n\nSome of the key resources mentioned in the report are:\n\n1. Congressional Research Service (CRS): An internal nonpartisan research and analysis agency of the United States Congress, providing members and committees with comprehensive, confidential, and objective research and analysis on a wide range of issues.\n2. Congressional Budget Office (CBO): An independent, nonpartisan agency within the legislative branch that provides budget and economic information to Congress.\n3. Government Accountability Office (GAO): The investigative arm of Congress, providing independent, nonpartisan reports on agency audits, policy analysis, and program evaluations.\n4. Government Publishing Office (GPO): The Federal Government's official, digital secure print shop, providing access to federal government information and documents.\n5. Law Library of Congress: A research library that offers legal research and reference services to congressional offices.\n6. National Archives and Records Administration (NARA): The nation's record keeper, preserving and making available to the public the records of the U.S. Government and its people.\n7. Office of Management and Budget (OMB): The implementation and enforcement arm of the President's policy agenda, providing copies of the President's federal budget proposals, proposed legislation, testimony, reports, and other documents.\n\nThe report also includes contact information and websites for these and other agencies, as well as relevant email lists and phone numbers. These resources can help congressional offices access the information and services they need to effectively carry out their duties.",
  "scored_prediction": "The report provides an extensive list of resources and contact information for various government agencies and offices that offer services and information relevant to congressional offices. These resources include libraries, research institutions, budget and accounting offices, and executive branch departments.\n\nSome of the key resources mentioned in the report are:\n\n1. Congressional Research Service (CRS): An internal nonpartisan research and analysis agency of the United States Congress, providing members and committees with comprehensive, confidential, and objective research and analysis on a wide range of issues.\n2. Congressional Budget Office (CBO): An independent, nonpartisan agency within the legislative branch that provides budget and economic information to Congress.\n3. Government Accountability Office (GAO): The investigative arm of Congress, providing independent, nonpartisan reports on agency audits, policy analysis, and program evaluations.\n4. Government Publishing Office (GPO): The Federal Government's official, digital secure print shop, providing access to federal government information and documents.\n5. Law Library of Congress: A research library that offers legal research and reference services to congressional offices.\n6. National Archives and Records Administration (NARA): The nation's record keeper, preserving and making available to the public the records of the U.S. Government and its people.\n7. Office of Management and Budget (OMB): The implementation and enforcement arm of the President's policy agenda, providing copies of the President's federal budget proposals, proposed legislation, testimony, reports, and other documents.\n\nThe report also includes contact information and websites for these and other agencies, as well as relevant email lists and phone numbers. These resources can help congressional offices access the information and services they need to effectively carry out their duties.",
  "score": 21.8181814173967,
  "answers": [
    "This report provides a brief list of key House, Senate, legislative support agencies, and executive branch offices, as well as links to online resources of use to new congressional staff who work with legislative procedures and conduct legislative research. Some of the websites listed are available only to congressional offices; other sites are restricted by chamber and are only available to those staff working in either House or Senate offices. This report is intended for congressional use only and will be updated annually."
  ],
